\nF6-90min-ex2-1.raw\nF6-90min-ex2-2.raw\nF6-90min-ex2-3.raw\nF7-90min-ex2-1.raw\nF7-90min-ex2-2.raw\nF7-90min-ex2-3.raw\nF8-90min-ex2-1.raw\nF8-90min-ex2-2.raw\nF8-90min-ex2-3.raw\nF9-90min-ex2-2.raw\nF9-90min-ex2-3.raw\nFITC-DSS.raw\nblank-label-free-quant-1.raw\nblank-label-free-quant-2.raw\nblank-label-free-quant-3.raw\nf22-90min-ex2-3.raw\nf3-90min-2.raw\nf3-90min-3.raw\nf3-90min.raw\nf4-90min-2.raw\nf5-90min.raw\nf9-90min-ex2-1.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nTargeted cross-linker delivery for in situ mapping of protein conformations and interactions in mitochondria\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCurrent methods for intracellular protein analysis mostly require the separation of specific organelles or the change of intracellular environment. However, the functions of these proteins are determined by their native microenvironment as they usually form complexes with ions, nucleic acids, and other proteins. Thus, we have explored a method for in situ crosslinking and mapping of mitochondrial proteins in living cells. Poly(lactic-co-glycolic acid) (PLGA) nanoparticles are functionalized with dihexadecyldimethylammonium bromide (DDAB) to deliver protein crosslinkers  into mitochondria. In situ crosslinked proteins are analyzed using mass spectrometry.\n\n[METHODS]\nCD-MS method consisted of four steps: 1) targeted delivery of the cross-linker to the mitochondria by the nanoparticle, 2) extraction and digestion of the proteins by ionic liquid filtration, which assisted sample preparation (i-FASP), 3) separation of the peptides by RPLC to reduce sample complexity, and 4) high-throughput and sensitive detection of peptides using a high-performance Q-Exactive instrument.An Easy-nano LC 1000 system coupled to a Q-Exactive mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, San Jose, CA) was used. Samples were automatically loaded onto a C18 RP trap column (150 μm i.d. × 3 cm) and separated by a C18 capillary column (150 μm i.d. × 15 cm), packed in-house with ReproSil-Pur C18-AQ particles (1.9 μm, 120 Å) with low-pH mobile phases (buffer A: 98% H2O + 2% ACN + 0.1% FA; buffer B: 2% H2O + 98% ACN + 0.1% FA). For unfractionated cross-linked peptides, the separation gradient was achieved by applying 3-12% B for 65min, 12-20% B for 65 min, 20-40% B for 5 min, 40-80% B for 1 min, and 80% B for 15 min. The mass spectrometry was operated in data-dependent mode. The full MS scans were performed by the Orbitrap at 70 000@200 resolving power within the scan range of 300-1800 m/z. The AGC target for the full scans was 3e6, and the maximum injection time was 80 ms. The loop count was 20, with the isolation window of 2.0 m/z. MS/MS scans were detected at the resolution of 17 500@200 with the fixed first mass of 110 m/z. The AGC target for the MS2 was 1e5, and the maximum injection time was 60 ms. Precursors were fragmented by higher-energy collision dissociation (HCD) with the normalized collision energy of 28. Only precursors with charge states 3-7 with an intensity higher than 1000 were selected for fragmentation, and the dynamic exclusion was set to 45 s. Each sample was analyzed in three times. For fractionated cross-linked peptides, the separation gradient was achieved by applying 2-7% B for 10s, 7-23% B for 50 min, 23-40% B for 20 min, 40-80% B for 2 min, and 80% B for 13 min. The AGC target for the MS2 was 5e4 and the dynamic exclusion was set to 18 s. The other parameter settings are the same as above. Each fractionation was analyzed in three times.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
