2026-06-30 15:28:10,155 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7d13ffbc-e6e5-43ec-9154-64c3e1ad1dd2', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nTargeted cross-linker delivery for in situ mapping of protein conformations and interactions in mitochondria\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCurrent methods for intracellular protein analysis mostly require the separation of specific organelles or the change of intracellular environment. However, the functions of these proteins are determined by their native microenvironment as they usually form complexes with ions, nucleic acids, and other proteins. Thus, we have explored a method for in situ crosslinking and mapping of mitochondrial proteins in living cells. Poly(lactic-co-glycolic acid) (PLGA) nanoparticles are functionalized with dihexadecyldimethylammonium bromide (DDAB) to deliver protein crosslinkers  into mitochondria. In situ crosslinked proteins are analyzed using mass spectrometry.\n\n[METHODS]\nCD-MS method consisted of four steps: 1) targeted delivery of the cross-linker to the mitochondria by the nanoparticle, 2) extraction and digestion of the proteins by ionic liquid filtration, which assisted sample preparation (i-FASP), 3) separation of the peptides by RPLC to reduce sample complexity, and 4) high-throughput and sensitive detection of peptides using a high-performance Q-Exactive instrument.An Easy-nano LC 1000 system coupled to a Q-Exactive mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, San Jose, CA) was used. Samples were automatically loaded onto a C18 RP trap column (150 μm i.d. × 3 cm) and separated by a C18 capillary column (150 μm i.d. × 15 cm), packed in-house with ReproSil-Pur C18-AQ particles (1.9 μm, 120 Å) with low-pH mobile phases (buffer A: 98% H2O + 2% ACN + 0.1% FA; buffer B: 2% H2O + 98% ACN + 0.1% FA). For unfractionated cross-linked peptides, the separation gradient was achieved by applying 3-12% B for 65min, 12-20% B for 65 min, 20-40% B for 5 min, 40-80% B for 1 min, and 80% B for 15 min. The mass spectrometry was operated in data-dependent mode. The full MS scans were performed by the Orbitrap at 70 000@200 resolving power within the scan range of 300-1800 m/z. The AGC target for the full scans was 3e6, and the maximum injection time was 80 ms. The loop count was 20, with the isolation window of 2.0 m/z. MS/MS scans were detected at the resolution of 17 500@200 with the fixed first mass of 110 m/z. The AGC target for the MS2 was 1e5, and the maximum injection time was 60 ms. Precursors were fragmented by higher-energy collision dissociation (HCD) with the normalized collision energy of 28. Only precursors with charge states 3-7 with an intensity higher than 1000 were selected for fragmentation, and the dynamic exclusion was set to 45 s. Each sample was analyzed in three times. For fractionated cross-linked peptides, the separation gradient was achieved by applying 2-7% B for 10s, 7-23% B for 50 min, 23-40% B for 20 min, 40-80% B for 2 min, and 80% B for 13 min. The AGC target for the MS2 was 5e4 and the dynamic exclusion was set to 18 s. The other parameter settings are the same as above. Each fractionation was analyzed in three times.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
